@scene(40)

// A post rising out of a ball set off its axis, the junction rounded with an
// exact fillet, which is shaded.
//
// unionRoundExactSteps(field1, field2, radius, steps) rounds the seam of a
// union with a rolling-ball fillet of that radius: the surface a ball of the
// radius sweeps as it rolls around the junction touching both solids. With the
// ball off the post's axis the seam is no circle and the fillet no torus; its
// section changes all the way round. The round finds the seam's point nearest
// p by Newton steps: where the seam is a circle (offset 0) three are exact to
// a millionth of a millimetre, and here the fillet moves by about 1e-4 of its
// volume between three steps and six, so about five settle it. The
// five-argument form, which takes the faces' normals and one step, is exact
// only where the faces are flat: where a sphere meets a cylinder its fillet is
// off by a few percent of the radius. Each step evaluates both fields again,
// so the fillet costs several times the one-step form to mesh, and more as the
// radius grows. Pass the step count as a constant.
//
// Try the sliders: radius sets the fillet, offset moves the ball off the axis.

ballRadius = 10;
postRadius = 4;
postHeight = 15;

@range(0, 10) radius = 3.96;

@range(0, 10) offset = 7;

ball = length(p - offset * vector.dirX) - ballRadius;
post = extrude(circleXY([0, 0], postRadius), 0, postHeight, intersectRound(0));

sharp = unionSharp(ball, post);
exact = unionRoundExactSteps(ball, post, radius, 3);
exact.color = (exact - sharp < 0 ? 0.6 : 1) * [1, 1, 1];

return exact;
